<div class="intro-content">
  <section>
    <h2>A shared framework for transformations</h2>

    <p>
      A parent axis names a broad typographic dimension that may change in a
      typeface – such as stroke thickness, proportion, spacing, or serif shape.
      It provides a shared framework for describing and comparing
      transformations across projects.
    </p>

    <p>
      A parent axis does not, however, prescribe how a typeface must be
      built. It describes the larger kind of change being considered, rather
      than a single fixed control or a complete design state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>When a parent axis becomes several child axes</h2>

    <p>
      A broad transformation can sometimes be applied consistently across a
      typeface. In other cases, different glyph groups require separate
      controls. Whether an axis is divided is a design decision, shaped by the
      behaviour of the forms involved.
    </p>

    <p>
      Child axes are these project-specific controls. They allow a typeface to
      distinguish between transformations that belong to the same broad
      typographic dimension but need different behaviour in different parts of
      the character set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Example: vertical proportion</h2>

    <p>
      <code>YTRA</code> describes vertical proportion as a broad parent
      transformation. In a particular typeface, uppercase, lowercase, and
      figures may not need to change in the same way or by the same amount.
    </p>

    <p>
      The project can therefore divide <code>YTRA</code> into separate child
      axes: <code>YTUC</code> for uppercase, <code>YTLC</code> for lowercase,
      and <code>YTFI</code> for figures. Each axis can respond to the
      structure of its own glyph group while remaining part of the larger
      vertical-proportion system.
    </p>

    <aside class="note">
      <p class="note-label">Example</p>
      <p>
        Dividing a parent axis does not mean that the resulting parameters
        are unrelated. The parameters describe different parts of one broader
        transformation, and are evaluated together when the typeface is used.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From parametric axes to parametric sources</h2>

    <p>
      A parametric axis defines what should change. Parametric sources define how
      that change is made in the glyphs. A source functions like a master for a
      specific transformation, recording the measurements, constructions, and
      outline adjustments needed to produce it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The aim is not complete independence between parameters. A change to one
      part of the design may require an adjustment elsewhere to preserve a
      stroke angle, proportion, rhythm, or overall colour. Parametric sources
      make those dependencies visible and available for testing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Blending</h2>

    <p>
      Child axes can be combined to produce blended axes or more familiar
      design states. Blending builds on the individual parametric
      transformations, rather than replacing their logic.
    </p>

    <p>
      This archive discusses blending separately. Here, the important point is
      that child axes preserve the individual decisions from which combined
      states are made.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A system, not a fixed taxonomy</h2>

    <p>
      Parent axes provide a framework for comparison, not a universal list
      that every project must implement. A project may divide, combine, or omit
      controls according to its design and source structure.
    </p>

    <p>
      Serif transformations provide one example. A project may need separate
      controls for horizontal and vertical serif dimensions, while another
      project may organise its serif forms differently. The relevant question
      is not whether every project uses the same parameters, but whether its
      parameters describe the design decisions clearly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to go next</h2>

    <p>
      Continue to the project documentation to see how parent and child
      axes are realised in a specific typeface. The reference material records
      the individual parameters, source data, mappings, and proof specimens.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where one parameter depends on another, the relevant pages link directly
      to each other. For example, an <code>XOPQ</code> adjustment in a
      single-stem glyph may require a related <a href="/reference/axes/xtra/">XTRA</a>
      adjustment to maintain the intended proportion.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
